Ideas and features we've agreed to leave for later. Add to it as they come up (`na add -x "…" --to NoCatchAllInRaiseScope`; `-x` leaves off the @na tag). Only what we mean to do next is tagged @na, so `na next` shows that and `na find --regex .` shows everything. Once an item is built, archive it with `na archive`: it moves to the Archive project with the date it was done.

NoCatchAllInRaiseScope:
- Handle a labelled runCatching block
	A runCatching whose block carries a label (a label name and at sign before the opening brace) wrapped around a raise isn't flagged. `runCatchingBody` in `NoCatchAllInRaiseScope.kt` casts the argument straight to `KtLambdaExpression`, so the labelled expression is skipped.
	Running it through `KtPsiUtil.safeDeparenthesize` first, as `argumentOf` already does, should fix it. Found in the second review on 2026-11-01. Cheap, so pick it up any time.
- Follow stored lambdas and local functions called inside the try
	Code inside the try that is called straight away isn't checked when it is a lambda stored in a val (`val t = { raise(e) }; t()`), a local function (`fun h() { raise(e) }; h()`) or an immediately invoked lambda (`{ raise(e) }()`). `runsInPlace` treats local functions and stored lambdas as running later, which is right when they escape the try and wrong when they're called inside it.
	Fixing it means following where the lambda or function is called before the try ends. Pick it up if it turns up in real code.
- Follow a Raise passed through a collection
	A Raise reaching a lambda through a collection, such as `listOf(r).forEach { it.raise(e) }`, or `forEach { x -> with(x) { ... } }`, isn't flagged. `opensFreshRaise` only looks at Raise-typed receivers and arguments of the call, not a List of Raise, so it decides the lambda opens its own scope.
	Passing a Raise around in a collection is unusual. Pick it up only if it turns up in real code.
- Stop flagging Any members called on a Raise
	Calling `toString()` or another member of Any on the Raise inside the try is flagged, as is calling an inner-class constructor or a super member inside a class that implements Raise. `raisesOutside` checks the dispatch receiver by the type of the value passed, not the class that declares the member.
	Checking the declaring class instead would fix it. Rare, so pick it up only if someone hits it.
- Stop flagging stored crossinline lambdas and local object getters
	A crossinline lambda that is stored for later (`lazy { block() }` inside an inline function) and a property getter in a local object inside the try are both flagged, although neither runs inside the try. The rule flagged both before the 2026-11-01 rewrite too.
	Pick it up only if it turns up in real code.

New rules:
- NoNestedEither rule
	Flag a declared or inferred `Either<E, Either<...>>`, which usually means a missing `bind()`. A likely bug rather than an idiom.
	From the idea doc (https://claude.ai/artifact/B1DUsRYvotDnRQd9o25J4s, 2026-10-31), which says to build a rule once a code review finds the same pattern twice.
	Build it as "Adding a rule" in README.md describes, with a message that says what to write instead. Check declared types and inferred ones (property types, function return types and the Analysis API's `expressionType`), matching `arrow.core.Either` by class id.

Housekeeping:
- Guard against projects without Arrow
	`coversRaise` in `NoCatchAllInRaiseScope.kt` builds `arrow.core.raise.RaiseCancellationException` with `buildClassType`, and the rule checks types against `arrow.core.raise.Raise`. We haven't checked what happens in a project without Arrow on its classpath; the tests always have it. If it throws, it fails the consumer's whole detekt run, not just this rule.
	Guard with `findClass(RAISE_CANCELLATION) ?: return false` (and the same for Raise) and add a test with Arrow off the snippet classpath. Do it before the first release.
- Publish a release
	Consumers use `--include-build` today, so the detektPlugins line can't be committed in their build. Publish to Maven Central (the `io.github.leeturner` namespace verifies through the GitHub account) or GitHub Packages to start.
	Releases should track detekt's version, since a rules jar built for one detekt API may not load in another. The idea doc says to wait for detekt 2.0 final; the alternative is to pin and release per detekt version. Guard against projects without Arrow first.
	Once it's out, switch encore-web-api from the `--include-build` setup to the published jar: commit the detektPlugins line in its `build.gradle.kts`.
- Re-check against detekt 2.0 final
	The plugin is built and tested against detekt 2.0.0-alpha.6 only. When 2.0 final ships, confirm the RuleSetProvider service file name, `RequiresAnalysisApi`, `lintWithContext` and the `compile-test-snippets` property, and bump the Kotlin Gradle plugin in `build.gradle.kts` to the Kotlin version detekt's `kotlin-compiler` dependency uses (2.4.10 for alpha.6).
	Also check the Analysis API calls the rule depends on: `successfulFunctionCallOrNull`, `valueArgumentMapping`, `receiverParameter` and `contextArguments` (still marked experimental). They move between versions; `argumentMapping` was already deprecated in alpha.6. Do it before publishing a release.
- Run the rules over real Arrow codebases
	So far the rule has only seen probe files and its own tests. encore-web-api has no raise calls in `src/main`, so it proves the plugin loads but not that the rule is right on real code. Run it over encore and at least one other Arrow codebase and note the false positives.
	The likeliest sources of false positives are Raise scopes hidden inside lambdas (`parMap`, `withError`, `recover`), which arrow-intellij also had to fix (https://slack-chats.kotlinlang.org/t/23059856/i-got-the-following-warning-from-intellij-gt-raise-context-m), and the guess about which lambdas run later (see the README).
	Use `--include-build` and run `./gradlew --stop` in the consuming project first, or its Gradle daemon runs the old rule. Do it before the first release; every rule is on by default, so false positives reach consumers straight away.
- Add CI with a Kotlin version matrix
	`.github/workflows/build.yml` runs `./gradlew build` on JDK 25 for pushes to main and PRs. The Analysis API is tied to the Kotlin compiler, and people report detekt-test helpers failing after a Kotlin bump (https://slack-chats.kotlinlang.org/t/30300686/related-to-v2-again-i-am-getting-an-error-in-the-unit-tests-). The plugin compiles with Kotlin 2.4.10 while encore-web-api uses 2.4.21.
	Still to add: a check that the plugin loads in a build on each Kotlin version consumers use. Do it before the first release.

Archive:
- UseEnsure rule @done(2026-11-02 09:31)
	Flag `if (cond) raise(e)` with no else inside a Raise scope and suggest `ensure(!cond) { e }`. An idiom nudge, so inactive by default.
	From the idea doc (https://claude.ai/artifact/B1DUsRYvotDnRQd9o25J4s, 2026-10-31), which says to build a rule once a code review finds the same pattern twice. arrow-intellij already offers this in the editor (https://arrow-kt.io/community/blog/2025/04/22/arrow-intellij-0-3/); the value here is a build-time check that command-line agents see.
	Build it as "Adding a rule" in README.md describes, with a message that says what to write instead. To tell whether the raise is inside a Raise scope, use the declared-receiver check in `raisesOutside` (`NoCatchAllInRaiseScope.kt`). Raise scopes hide inside lambdas (`parMap`, `withError`, `recover`) and arrow-intellij had to fix the same kind of false positive (https://slack-chats.kotlinlang.org/t/23059856/i-got-the-following-warning-from-intellij-gt-raise-context-m), so run it over real code before relying on it (see "Run the rules over real Arrow codebases").
- UseEnsureNotNull rule @done(2026-11-02 09:37)
	Flag `if (x == null) raise(e)` inside a Raise scope and suggest `ensureNotNull(x) { e }`, which also smart-casts x. Leave `x ?: raise(e)` alone, since it's idiomatic Kotlin. Inactive by default. `UseEnsure` skips `x == null` and `null == x` conditions so the two rules don't report the same line.
	From the idea doc (https://claude.ai/artifact/B1DUsRYvotDnRQd9o25J4s, 2026-10-31), which says to build a rule once a code review finds the same pattern twice. arrow-intellij already offers this in the editor (https://arrow-kt.io/community/blog/2025/04/22/arrow-intellij-0-3/).
	Build it as "Adding a rule" in README.md describes, with a message that says what to write instead. To tell whether the raise is inside a Raise scope, use `raiseReceivers` (`Raise.kt`), as `UseEnsure` does. Raise scopes hide inside lambdas (`parMap`, `withError`, `recover`) and arrow-intellij had to fix the same kind of false positive (https://slack-chats.kotlinlang.org/t/23059856/i-got-the-following-warning-from-intellij-gt-raise-context-m), so run it over real code before relying on it (see "Run the rules over real Arrow codebases").
- PreferEitherCatch rule @done(2026-11-02 10:18)
	Flag `either { catch({ ... }) { raise(f(it)) } }` and suggest `Either.catch { ... }.mapLeft(f)`.
	From the idea doc (https://claude.ai/artifact/B1DUsRYvotDnRQd9o25J4s, 2026-10-31), which says to build a rule once a code review finds the same pattern twice. encore-web-api had this pattern 9 times before its review.
	Build it as "Adding a rule" in README.md describes, with a message that says what to write instead. To tell whether the raise is inside a Raise scope, use `raiseReceivers` (`Raise.kt`), as `UseEnsure` does. Raise scopes hide inside lambdas (`parMap`, `withError`, `recover`) and arrow-intellij had to fix the same kind of false positive (https://slack-chats.kotlinlang.org/t/23059856/i-got-the-following-warning-from-intellij-gt-raise-context-m), so run it over real code before relying on it (see "Run the rules over real Arrow codebases"). Match `catch` by its resolved callable id (`arrow.core.raise.catch`), not its name.
- NoFoldBackIntoEither rule @done(2026-11-02 10:42)
	Flag `.fold({ Left(f(it)) }, { Right(g(it)) })` and suggest `.mapLeft(f).map(g)`.
	From the idea doc (https://claude.ai/artifact/B1DUsRYvotDnRQd9o25J4s, 2026-10-31), which says to build a rule once a code review finds the same pattern twice.
	Build it as "Adding a rule" in README.md describes, with a message that says what to write instead. Match `fold` resolving to Arrow's `Either.fold`, with lambdas whose last expression builds a `Left` and a `Right` (including `.left()` and `.right()`).
